Resonance

License: GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later (/LICENSE)

Resonance is a high-fidelity simulator and regression harness for
SUT binaries: it runs real binaries in isolated network namespaces
over a simulated radio mesh, and asserts cluster convergence across
partition, fault, and scale scenarios. Concord
(https://github.com/podomy/concord) is one SUT; weld with ROS peers
is the other. Scenarios are YAML files run by bin/rsuite; no
SUT-specific binary ships with the harness.

Scope

  Concord reference matrix is consolidated, not expanding. INVARIANTS
  names the pinned behaviors and check/ holds their frozen
  enforcement. The mesh stays the only channel between nodes.
  All work goes inward:
  correctness, reliability, verification, and optimization of what
  is already here. No new surface, fixes only.

  The bar, same for every change: externally identical behavior.
  Optimization needs a measurement before and after, not a hunch.
  Replacement needs the same wire format, CLI surface, and journal
  semantics, proven by the existing gate plus a Resonance run, not
  by reading the diff. Fixes need a failing gate first, then the
  smallest patch that turns it green without moving any other
  number. Cleanup needs the full gate green on both sides; elegance
  in the diff proves nothing. Anything adding a flag, a verb, or a
  field is expansion, and the answer is no until the freeze lifts.

Build and test

  make                    # build rsuite (test bins build transiently under test)
  make test               # run all tests plus lint plus standard, no privs
  make lint               # validate suite/examples/*.yaml, no privileges
  make standard           # C23 spelling plus version gate, no privileges
  make fuzzer             # ASan parser fuzz over YAML, needs sanitizer
  make img-build          # build test image bins offline, needs Go
  make clean              # remove binaries and test artifacts
  make compile-commands   # refresh clang tooling input, no privileges

`make` builds bin/rsuite. It
leaves out bin/fuzzer, which needs ASan plus UBSan runtimes that
are absent on some hosts. Build that one with `make fuzzer`.
`make test` is the single unprivileged entry point: it runs every
test binary (TUN tests skip without privileges), then lint,
then standard, then the Go suite when Go exists, else it prints a
skip line and stays green. C-only boxes pass without Go; concord
and img paths need Go.

YAML scenarios run through bin/rsuite (built with make bin/rsuite
via the bin/rsuite target):

  bin/rsuite lint <file.yaml>...    # validate without running
  bin/rsuite run <file.yaml>...     # run (needs root for netns + TUN)
  bin/rsuite list <file.yaml>...    # parse-only summary, no privileges
  bin/rsuite doctor                 # report on setup and dependencies

Every make target above exists in the Makefile: all (the bare make),
test, lint, standard, fuzzer,
img-build, clean, compile-commands, plus the file targets
bin/rsuite, and every test binary. `log` and `check` are retired
stubs that fail loudly. The .PHONY list
names every phony target, so a file named lint never shadows
the lint target.

Scenario files live in suite/examples/*.yaml (minimal examples
only); full suites live in the repos under test, not here.

Documentation

  Start at /docs/overview. It explains the six layers every
  scenario is built from, one file per layer (/docs/layer0-sim,
  /docs/layer1-mesh, /docs/layer2-lifecycle,
  /docs/layer3-faults, /docs/layer4-observation,
  /docs/layer5-runner), plus a separate note on test speed
  (/docs/speed) and a glossary of terms (/docs/glossary).
  docs/yaml is the authoritative YAML operation reference.
  docs/benchmark is the canonical benchmark measurement reference.
  New keys are defined in the matching docs/yaml sections.

Contributing

Discuss your change with the engineering team at contact@podomy.com
before opening a PR in order not to waste anybody's effort or time.

Announcements and engineering updates on distributed systems, consensus
algorithms, and robotic fleet coordination are shared via our newsletter
at https://podomy.com.

  Commit message format - /COMMITS
  Contributor license agreement - /CLA
  Contributing - /CONTRIBUTING

